\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Structure of ResNet-18 for a $224\times 224$ input. Each row is one layer or a group of identical consecutive layers. Kernel gives the dimensions of one kernel, Num the number of kernels, and $s$ and $p$ the stride and padding (Equation~\ref {eq:conv_output_size}). Shortcut rows are not counted in the 18 layers. In each block, the second ReLU is applied after the shortcut is added. Params includes batch normalisation. The parameter counts in this and the following tables are from torchvision \citep {torchvision2016}, with the 1000-class ImageNet output layer.}}{7}{table.caption.11}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Structure of DenseNet-121 for a $224\times 224$ input, with columns as in Table~\ref {tab:resnet_layers}. Each dense layer is a $1\times 1$ convolution followed by a $3\times 3$ convolution, and a range such as 64--224 means that the number of input channels grows by $k=32$ with each dense layer. Batch normalisation and ReLU precede each convolution and the global average pool, except for convolution 1, which they follow.}}{8}{table.caption.12}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Structure of EfficientNet-B0 for a $224\times 224$ input. Each row is one layer, one MBConv block, or a group of identical consecutive blocks. For a block, Kernel and $s$ refer to the depthwise convolution, while Expanded is the number of channels after the expansion. MBConv6 has an expansion ratio of $t=6$, and MBConv1 has no expansion convolution. For the two ordinary convolutions, the number of kernels equals the number of output channels.}}{9}{table.caption.13}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Structure of ConvNeXt-Tiny for a $224\times 224$ input, with columns as in Table~\ref {tab:efficientnet_layers}. For a block, Kernel refers to the depthwise convolution and Expanded is the number of channels after the first $1\times 1$ convolution. Layer normalisation follows the stem convolution and precedes each downsampling convolution and the FC layer.}}{10}{table.caption.14}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Summary of the five models used in this project, ordered by year of publication, with their number of layers and parameter counts in millions. Layers counts the convolutional and fully-connected layers on the main path of each network, so shortcut convolutions and the fully-connected layers inside squeeze-and-excitation blocks are not counted. The clarifier and aerobic zone columns include the tuned classifier head of each final model.}}{10}{table.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
